---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Your data lives where you can write — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="Why LightPhotos keeps ratings, edits and thumbnails in a folder next to your photos instead of in a database, and how a browser's permission rules decided it for me."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-08-10">August 10, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Your data lives where you are allowed to write</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          Why ratings, edits and thumbnails sit in a folder next to your photos
          instead of in a database, and how a browser's permission rules made
          that decision for me.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 4 min read</p>
      </header>

      <p>
        LightPhotos has no database. Rate a photo four stars and it writes a
        small JSON file called <code>IMG_4471.jpg.xmp</code> into a hidden
        <code>.lightphotos</code> folder, sitting right next to
        <code>IMG_4471.jpg</code>. That is the whole persistence layer.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not how the first version worked, and it is not a design I would
        have defended in a review. I got here by being forced into it twice.
      </p>

      <h2>My first attempt</h2>
      <p>
        One catalog for the whole app, keyed by each photo's full path, sitting
        in the usual application support directory. It is the standard answer
        and it works fine for a while.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also breaks in exactly the way people use a photo tool. People move
        folders. They import a card into <code>~/Desktop/import</code>, cull it,
        then drag the folder onto an external drive. The moment that happens,
        every path in the catalog points at nothing.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 246" role="img" aria-label="Comparison: a central catalog keyed by full path breaks when a folder moves, while sidecar files inside the folder travel with the photos.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">V1 &middot; CENTRAL CATALOG</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="22" width="300" height="208" rx="10"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="50" y="44" width="200" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="150" y="63" text-anchor="middle">catalog.db in ~/Library</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="150" y1="74" x2="150" y2="112"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" d="M142 85 L158 101 M158 85 L142 101"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="168" y="97">path points at nothing</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="50" y="118" width="200" height="54" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-t" x="64" y="139">Iceland/</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="80" y="159">IMG_4471.jpg</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="150" y="194" text-anchor="middle">folder moved to an external drive</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="150" y="212" text-anchor="middle">an hour of culling, gone</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="340" y="12">V2 &middot; BESIDE THE PHOTOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="340" y="22" width="300" height="208" rx="10"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="370" y="44" width="240" height="118" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-t" x="384" y="66">Iceland/</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="400" y="88">IMG_4471.jpg</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="400" y="110">.lightphotos/</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="408" y="118" width="190" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-t" x="418" y="136">IMG_4471.jpg.xmp</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="490" y1="162" x2="490" y2="176"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M484 176 L496 176 L490 186 Z"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="490" y="204" text-anchor="middle">move it, copy it, back it up</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="490" y="222" text-anchor="middle">the ratings come with it</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The fix was not a repair tool. It was putting the data where the photo
          already is.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Putting it next to the photos</h2>
      <p>
        Now moving the folder moves your work. Copying it to a colleague copies
        your work. Backing it up backs up your work. I did not write any code
        for any of that. The data and the thing it describes are in the same
        place, so it comes for free.
      </p>
      <p>Three details make it liveable.</p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Created on write, never on read.</strong> Browsing someone
          else's photos leaves nothing behind.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Written through a temporary file, then renamed.</strong> A
          rename is atomic on every filesystem worth using, so a crash halfway
          through leaves the previous version intact instead of a truncated
          file.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The original photo is never touched.</strong> A tool whose job
          is deciding what to keep should not modify the thing you might keep.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Then the browser made the decision for me</h2>
      <p>
        Thumbnails are expensive, so they get cached on disk. In the Mac-only
        days that cache lived in <code>~/Library/Caches</code>, exactly where
        Apple tells you to put it. Then I ported the app to run in a browser
        tab.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 160" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: a browser tab may read and write only inside the folder the user picked; the system cache directory and the rest of the disk are unreachable.">
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="62" width="150" height="50" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="75" y="92" text-anchor="middle">Browser tab</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M150 87 C 220 87, 230 37, 296 37 M150 87 H296 M150 87 C 220 87, 230 137, 296 137"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" d="M215 46 L231 62 M231 46 L215 62 M215 124 L231 140 M231 124 L215 140"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M296 31 L296 43 L308 37 Z M296 81 L296 93 L308 87 Z M296 131 L296 143 L308 137 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="312" y="20" width="328" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="476" y="41" text-anchor="middle">~/Library/Caches &mdash; does not exist here</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="312" y="70" width="328" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="476" y="91" text-anchor="middle">The one folder you picked</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="312" y="120" width="328" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="476" y="141" text-anchor="middle">Anywhere else on disk &mdash; no permission</text>

        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Read and write inside the folder you picked. That is the whole
          permission, so the browser build would have re-decoded every
          thumbnail on every page load, forever.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        In a browser tab, a cache in the system cache directory does not perform
        badly. It cannot exist.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        So the thumbnail cache moved into <code>.lightphotos</code>, next to the
        ratings that were already there. I did it because the browser left me no
        choice. It turned out to be better everywhere.
      </p>

      <h2>What I got out of it</h2>
      <p>
        Moving a folder now carries its thumbnails as well as its ratings. Plug
        the drive into another machine and the grid fills instantly instead of
        spending a minute re-decoding everything.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Mac build and the browser build also share one cache, which took one
        deliberate compromise. The cache key is built from the file's size and
        modification time, rounded to milliseconds, because milliseconds is all
        a browser will tell you. The Mac's nanosecond precision would have made
        the two builds compute different keys for the same untouched file, each
        blind to the other's work.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is one entry per photo, so a folder's cache is bounded by the
        number of photos in it and needs no eviction policy. I just sweep for
        dead entries when a folder opens. If the app cannot write to a folder,
        it decodes fresh every session and never tells you the write failed. A
        cache you cannot write to is an inconvenience, not an error.
      </p>

      <h2>What it cost me</h2>
      <p>
        Records are keyed by filename alone, because the folder is implied by
        where the file sits. That makes cross-folder work dangerous. If you
        switch folders mid-job, a result arriving late would get written against
        a photo with the same name in the new folder. So I abandon any batch job
        the moment the folder changes.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is also no cheap way to ask a question across every folder you
        have ever opened. "Show me everything I rated five stars this year"
        would mean walking all of them. A central database answers that in a
        millisecond. If that question were core to the product, this would be
        the wrong design.
      </p>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Where data lives decides which operations stay cheap.</strong>
          Next to the thing it describes makes moving, copying and sharing free,
          and makes cross-cutting queries expensive. A central store does the
          reverse.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The most restricted platform is the best design input you will
          get.</strong> I did not choose to co-locate the cache. A browser's
          permission model refused to let me do anything else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Design for the sandbox first, not last.</strong> The version
          in the system cache directory worked fine and nobody was complaining,
          so I would never have argued my way to the better design on my own.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the cost down where someone will trip over it.</strong>
          Filename-only keys make late background results dangerous. That
          warning belongs on the function that has to handle it.
        </li>
      </ul>


